tel 1.0

base nomenclature
import soundness

info   names whose validity rules are part of their type, checked as the code compiles and as text arrives
detail
    _Nomenclature_ represents a name — a filename, a DOM id, a CSS class, a Git branch, a test
    id — as a `Name[plane]`: text which is known to satisfy the rules of the namespace, or
    **plane**, it belongs to. A plane is a marker type with a `Nominative` given which states
    its rules as an intersection of constraint types, `MustNotContain["/"] & MustNotEqual[".."]`,
    and a `Name` for that plane exists only if its text satisfies every one of them: a literal
    written with the `n"…"` interpolator is checked while the code compiles, and text arriving
    at runtime becomes a `Name` through `Name[plane](text)` or `text.as[Name[plane]]`, which
    raise a `Name.Error` naming the one rule it broke. Holding a `Name[plane]` is therefore
    proof that it is valid in that plane.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    sealed trait Tag
    inline given Tag is Nominative under MustNotStart["0"] & MustNotContain["."] & MustNotEqual[""] = !!

    val tag: Name[Tag] = n"release"
    val checked: Name[Tag] = Name[Tag](t"v2")
    capture[Name.Error](Name[Tag](t"0hello")).message.show
    // t"the name 0hello is not valid because it must not start with 0"
    ```

    A `Name` is a subtype of `Text`, so it is passed wherever text is and compares equal to
    the text it wraps; it is covariant in its plane, so a `Name[A & B]` serves where a
    `Name[A]` or a `Name[B]` is required, which is how one `n"…"` literal takes the type of
    every plane in scope whose rules it satisfies. The rules are `Rule` objects paired with
    `Check` types — `MustStart`, `MustNotStart`, `MustEnd`, `MustNotEnd`, `MustContain`,
    `MustNotContain`, `MustNotEqual`, `MustMatch` and `MustNotMatch`, each parameterised by a
    literal — and three predicate rules parameterised by their own description instead:
    `JavaIdentifier`, `CssIdentifier` and `DomIdentifier`. A rule knows how to describe itself,
    so the compile error for a bad literal and the runtime error for bad input say the same
    thing, in the same words. The planes `DomId`, `CssClass` and `Required` are built in; the
    filesystem planes of galilei (`Linux`, `Windows`, `MacOs`, …), the `Classpath` plane of
    hellenism and the `Probing` plane of probably's test ids are defined the same way.

    The library has two source sets:

    - `nomenclature.core` — `Name`, `n"…"`, `Nominative`, `Rule`, `Check`, the `Must…` rules,
      the identifier rules and the three built-in planes. It compiles for the JVM, Scala.js and
      Scala Native.
    - `nomenclature.lexicon` — `Moniker` and `Vocabulary`: human-friendly names of the shape
      `brilliant-leopard` for small integers, built from word lists read from any streamable
      source.

    A `Nominative` given is declared `inline given … = !!`: the macros behind `n"…"` and
    `Name[plane](…)` read only its *type*, so no instance is ever constructed, and `!!` is the
    erased placeholder. Since `import pkg.*` does not import givens, a plane declared in a
    library's companion object is found through implicit scope, while one declared at the top
    of a package has to be imported by name. Everything is exported to the `soundness`
    umbrella.

refer nomenclature.Name
refer nomenclature.Nominative
refer nomenclature.Rule
refer nomenclature.Moniker

entry .Name   text proven valid in a naming plane: `Name[plane]`, a subtype of `Text`
  detail
      `Name[plane]` is the opaque type `nomenclature.internal.Name[plane]`, exported here. It is
      a `Text` which has passed every rule of `plane`, where `plane` is a type with a
      `Nominative` given in implicit scope. A name is made in three ways: `n"…"` writes a
      literal and checks it against every plane in scope while the code compiles;
      `Name[plane](text)` checks runtime text against one plane's rules and raises `Name.Error`
      if a rule fails; and `text.as[Name[plane]]` is the same check through the `Decodable`
      typeclass. A name which exists is valid, so code which takes a `Name[Linux]` never
      re-validates it. The companion's members — `Name.apply`, `Name.verify`, its givens,
      `Name.Error` and `Name.Extractor` — are documented under `nomenclature.internal.Name`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      sealed trait Branch
      inline given Branch is Nominative under MustNotContain[" "] & MustNotStart["-"] = !!

      val literal: Name[Branch] = n"main"
      val checked: Name[Branch] = Name[Branch](t"feature/x")
      val decoded: Name[Branch] = t"hotfix".as[Name[Branch]]
      val text: Text = checked
      checked == t"feature/x"
      ```

      `Name` is covariant in its plane, so `Name[A & B]` is a `Name[A]` and a `Name[B]`: a
      name valid in several planes at once has their intersection as its plane. Two planes
      with identical rules are still different types, so a `Name[Branch]` is not accepted
      where a `Name[Tag]` is required.

      The rules are checked in the order the plane's intersection writes them, and the first
      one violated is the one reported, so an error names a single concrete fault. The check
      is run at compile time for a literal and at runtime for text, by the same `Rule.check`
      predicates, so the two never disagree.
  refer nomenclature.n
  refer nomenclature.Nominative
  refer nomenclature.Rule

entry .n   the `n"…"` interpolator: a name literal, checked against the planes in scope as the code compiles
    `n"main"` is a `Name` whose plane is the intersection of every plane with a `Nominative`
    given in scope whose rules the literal satisfies, so one literal serves wherever any of
    those planes is required, and it is a compile error if no plane in scope accepts it: _the
    name `.` is not a valid identifier in any namespace in scope_. With an expected type
    `Name[Tag]`, a literal which breaks one of `Tag`'s rules fails to conform, since `Tag` is
    not among the planes it was found valid in.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    sealed trait Tag
    inline given Tag is Nominative under MustNotStart["0"] & MustNotContain["."] = !!

    val tag: Name[Tag] = n"release"
    val bad: Name[Tag] = n"0hello"   // does not compile
    ```

    The interpolator takes no `$`-substitutions, since the text must be known as the code
    compiles; text known only at runtime is checked with `Name[plane](text)`. The literal's
    characters are taken verbatim, without escape processing. As a pattern, `case n"main" =>`
    matches a `Name` equal to the literal, and the literal is checked against the scrutinee's
    plane first.

    Which planes are in scope decides what `n"…"` infers: a plane declared in a companion
    object is always visible through implicit scope, and the umbrella's own planes — the
    filesystem planes, `Classpath`, `Probing` — are among the candidates, so an unascribed
    `n"…"` has a wide intersection type. Tightening a plane's rules narrows what it accepts:

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label One rule: a tag may not be empty
      code
          locally:
            sealed trait Tag
            inline given Tag is Nominative under MustNotEqual[""] = !!
            val tag: Name[Tag] = n"v1.0-beta"
    frame
      label Two rules: nor may it contain a dot
      code
          locally:
            sealed trait Tag
            inline given Tag is Nominative under MustNotEqual[""] & MustNotContain["."] = !!
            val tag: Name[Tag] = n"v1-beta"
            val dotted: Name[Tag] = n"v1.0-beta"   // does not compile
    frame
      label Three rules: and it must match a pattern
      code
          locally:
            sealed trait Tag
            inline given Tag is Nominative under
                MustNotEqual[""] & MustNotContain["."] & MustMatch["v[0-9]+(-[a-z]+)?"] = !!
            val tag: Name[Tag] = n"v1-beta"
            val capital: Name[Tag] = n"v1-Beta"   // does not compile
    ##
    ```

entry .Nominative   the typeclass which gives a plane its rules: `plane is Nominative under rules`
  detail
      A `Nominative` is a `Typeclass` whose `Self` is the plane and whose `Limit` (from
      `Limited`, written with the `under` alias) is the plane's rules: an intersection of
      `Check` types, each a rule applied to a literal, such as
      `MustNotContain["/"] & MustNotEqual["."] & MustNotEqual[".."] & MustNotEqual[""]`. The
      macros behind `n"…"`, `Name[plane](text)` and `text.as[Name[plane]]` summon the given
      for a plane and read its `Limit` type to find which rules to run, in the order they
      are written.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      sealed trait Branch
      object Branch:
        type Rules = MustNotContain[" "] & MustNotStart["-"] & MustNotEnd["/"]
        inline given nominative: Branch is Nominative under Rules = !!
      ```

      The given is declared `inline given … = !!`: only its type is ever read, so no instance
      is constructed and `!!` stands in for the value. It belongs in the plane's companion,
      where implicit scope finds it with no import; a plane with a `Rules` alias, as the
      filesystem planes have, lets the same rules be reused by a `Path`'s element type. A
      rule whose parameter is not a string literal type cannot be evaluated at compile time:
      `n"…"` skips such a plane, and `Name[plane](text)` and `Name.verify` report it as a bad
      type.
  refer nomenclature.Name
  refer nomenclature.Check
  entry .apply   `Nominative[plane, rules]()`: a runtime instance, for code which needs a value
      Makes a `plane is Nominative under rules` instance. The macros read a given's type and
      never its value, so a plane's given is usually `!!`; this constructor is for a given
      which must be a real value, as when it is passed explicitly.

entry .Rule   what a constraint object is: a predicate on a name, and a phrase describing it
  detail
      Every constraint, `MustStart`, `MustNotContain`, `CssIdentifier` and the rest, is an
      object extending `Rule` with two things: `#check`, a predicate of the name and the rule's
      parameter, and `#describe`, which turns the parameter into the phrase the error uses,
      _must start with x-_. Alongside the object is a sealed trait of the same name extending
      `Check[parameter]`, which is the type written in a plane's rules; the macros find the
      object as the trait's companion and call `check` on it, at compile time for a literal
      and at runtime for text.

      A new rule is the pair:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object MustBeShort extends Rule({ limit => m"must be no longer than $limit characters" }, (name, limit) => name.length <= limit.s.toInt)
      sealed trait MustBeShort[limit <: Label] extends Check[limit]

      sealed trait Handle
      inline given Handle is Nominative under MustBeShort["12"] & MustNotContain[" "] = !!
      ```

      The description is part of the rule rather than written at each use, so the compile
      error for a bad literal and the runtime `Name.Error` say the same thing. A rule's
      parameter is always a string literal type, which is read as text; a rule which has no
      natural parameter, as `JavaIdentifier` has none, takes the description to use in the
      error as its parameter instead.
  refer nomenclature.Check
  refer nomenclature.Nominative
  entry #describe   the phrase a violation is reported with, from the rule's parameter
      A `Text => Message` function: for `MustNotStart` and the parameter `0`, _must not start
      with 0_. `Name.Error`'s message is _the name … is not valid because it_ followed by
      this phrase.
  entry #check   whether a name satisfies the rule: `(name, parameter) => Boolean`
      The predicate, taking the candidate text and the rule's parameter. It is called at
      runtime by `Name[plane](text)` and at compile time, by the macro, for an `n"…"` literal.

entry .Check   the type of a rule applied to its parameter: `MustStart["x-"]` is a `Check["x-"]`
    The trait a rule's sealed trait extends, parameterised by the rule's literal parameter. A
    plane's rules are an intersection of `Check`s, and the macros take the intersection apart
    into its `Check` members, read each one's parameter as a string constant, and find the
    `Rule` object which shares its name. A `Check` is never instantiated: it is a type-level
    description only.

entry .MustStart   the rule that a name begins with the given text: `MustStart["x-"]`
    _must start with x-_. The whole prefix must be at the beginning: `x-ray` and `x-` alone
    pass `MustStart["x-"]`, and `ray-x-` does not.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    sealed trait Prefixed
    inline given Prefixed is Nominative under MustStart["x-"] = !!

    Name[Prefixed](t"x-ray")
    capture[Name.Error](Name[Prefixed](t"ray")).message.show
    // t"the name ray is not valid because it must start with x-"
    ```

entry .MustNotStart   the rule that a name does not begin with the given text: `MustNotStart["0"]`
    _must not start with 0_. The complement of `MustStart`: a name whose first characters
    are the parameter fails, and any other name passes, including one which contains the
    text elsewhere.

entry .MustEnd   the rule that a name ends with the given text: `MustEnd["!"]`
    _must end with !_. A name whose last characters are the parameter passes; `hello!9`
    fails `MustEnd["!"]`.

entry .MustNotEnd   the rule that a name does not end with the given text: `MustNotEnd["z"]`
    _must not end with z_. `quartz` fails `MustNotEnd["z"]`; `zebra` and `quartz-a` pass,
    since the text is forbidden only at the end.

entry .MustContain   the rule that a name contains the given text anywhere: `MustContain["-"]`
    _must contain -_. The text may be at the start, in the middle or at the end: `main-nav`,
    `-nav` and `main-` all pass `MustContain["-"]`, and `mainnav` does not.

entry .MustNotContain   the rule that a name contains the given text nowhere: `MustNotContain["/"]`
    _must not contain /_. The rule the filesystem planes use to forbid a separator inside a
    path element, and a DOM id uses to forbid a space.

entry .MustNotEqual   the rule that a name is not exactly the given text: `MustNotEqual[".."]`
    _must not equal .._, or, for the empty parameter `MustNotEqual[""]`, _must not be empty_.
    The common form of a non-emptiness rule, and of excluding the special names a namespace
    reserves: the filesystem planes write `MustNotEqual["."] & MustNotEqual[".."] &
    MustNotEqual[""]`. There is no `MustEqual`, since a plane with one valid name is not a
    namespace.

entry .MustMatch   the rule that a name matches a regular expression in full: `MustMatch["[a-z]+"]`
    _must match [a-z]+_. The parameter is a Java regular expression, and the match is
    anchored at both ends, as `String.matches` is: `MustMatch["a+"]` accepts `aaa` and
    rejects `baa` and `aab`, where the pattern is found only within the name. The empty name
    fails a one-or-more pattern. This is the rule to reach for when a namespace's alphabet
    is a character class: probably's test tags are `MustMatch["[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*"]`.

entry .MustNotMatch   the rule that a name does not match a regular expression in full: `MustNotMatch["[0-9]+"]`
    _must not match [0-9]+_. The complement of `MustMatch`, with the same anchoring: `123`
    fails `MustNotMatch["[0-9]+"]`, and `1a3` passes, since the pattern does not match the
    whole name. The `Classpath` plane uses `MustNotMatch["[0-9].*"]` to forbid a leading
    digit.

entry .JavaIdentifier   the rule that a name is a Java identifier, parameterised by its description
    _must be a valid Java identifier_, for `JavaIdentifier["a valid Java identifier"]`. The
    check is `java.lang.Character`'s definition: nonempty, an identifier-start character
    followed by identifier-part characters; keywords are not excluded. Unlike the `Must…`
    rules, the parameter is not something to compare against but the phrase the error
    should use, which is the shape a rule takes when its check is a predicate of its own
    rather than a comparison with a literal. It is the rule of probably's `Probing` plane,
    the plane of test ids.

entry .CssIdentifier   the rule that a name is a CSS identifier, parameterised by its description
    _must be a valid CSS identifier_, for `CssIdentifier["a valid CSS identifier"]`. The
    check is the CSS Syntax Module Level 3 `<ident-token>` grammar without escapes: an
    optional leading `-` or `--`, an identifier-start character (a letter, `_` or any
    non-ASCII character), then identifier characters, which also admit digits and `-`. A lone
    `-`, a leading digit and an escape sequence are all rejected. It is the rule of the
    `CssClass` plane.

entry .DomIdentifier   the rule that a name is an HTML element id, parameterised by its description
    _must be a valid DOM id_, for `DomIdentifier["a valid DOM id"]`. The check is the HTML
    Living Standard's: at least one character and no ASCII whitespace (tab, newline, form
    feed, carriage return or space). It is the rule of the `DomId` plane.

entry .CssClass   the plane of CSS class names: `Name[CssClass]` is a valid CSS identifier
  detail
      A marker type with the single rule `CssIdentifier["a valid CSS identifier"]`, so a
      `Name[CssClass]` is text which can follow `.` in a selector. honeycomb's HTML elements
      take their classes as `Name[CssClass]`s, which is how a misspelt class literal fails to
      compile.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val cls: Name[CssClass] = n"main-nav"
      capture[Name.Error](Name[CssClass](t"1col")).message.show
      // t"the name 1col is not valid because it must be a valid CSS identifier"
      ```
  refer nomenclature.CssIdentifier
  entry .nominative   the given which holds CSS class names to `CssIdentifier`
      In the companion, so in implicit scope wherever `CssClass` is named.

entry .DomId   the plane of DOM element ids: `Name[DomId]` is a valid HTML id
  detail
      A marker type with the single rule `DomIdentifier["a valid DOM id"]`: nonempty, with
      no ASCII whitespace. honeycomb's `id` attributes take a `Name[DomId]`, and a DOM id is
      `Focusable`, so it can be the target of a `focus` as the CSS selector `#id`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val id: Name[DomId] = n"main-content"
      capture[Name.Error](Name[DomId](t"a b")).message.show
      // t"the name a b is not valid because it must be a valid DOM id"
      ```
  refer nomenclature.DomIdentifier
  entry .nominative   the given which holds DOM ids to `DomIdentifier`
      In the companion, so in implicit scope wherever `DomId` is named.
  entry .focusable   a DOM id focuses its element as the CSS selector `#id`
      The `Focusable` given for `Name[DomId]`, with the strategy `css selector` and the focus
      text `#` followed by the id, for the libraries which locate an element on a page by a
      selector.

entry .Required   the plane whose only rule is non-emptiness: `Name[Required]` is any nonempty text
    A marker type with the single rule `MustNotEqual[""]`, for the common case where "not
    empty" is the whole of what a field requires. Its `Nominative` given is anonymous, in
    the companion, so it is in implicit scope wherever `Required` is named.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    val title: Name[Required] = n"Untitled"
    capture[Name.Error](Name[Required](t"")).message.show
    // t"the name “” is not valid because it must not be empty"
    ```

entry .disintersect   `disintersect[A & B & C]`: an intersection type taken apart into a tuple type
    A `transparent inline` method whose result is a value of the tuple type `(A, B, C)` for
    the intersection `A & B & C`, and `EmptyTuple` for a type which is not an intersection.
    It is the type-level operation the macros use to walk a plane's rules, exposed for code
    which needs to enumerate the members of an intersection type; the value itself is
    `null` cast to the tuple type and must not be used, only its type.

entry .staticCompanion   `staticCompanion[T]`: the companion object of a type, as a value
    A `transparent inline` method which resolves to the companion object of `T`, typed as
    `Matchable`: `staticCompanion[MustStart[""]]` is the `MustStart` object. It is how the
    macros reach a `Rule` from a `Check` type, exposed for code which needs the same.

entry .internal   the opaque `Name` type and its companion, which the `nomenclature` package exports
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `Name` lives here so that its representation as `Text` is hidden
      from the rest of the package, and is exported as `nomenclature.Name`, whose page has the
      full account. Its members are `Name.apply`, `Name.verify`, the givens `encodable`,
      `inspectable` and `decodable`, `Name.Error` and `Name.Extractor`.
  entry .Name   text proven valid in a plane; see `nomenclature.Name`
    detail
        The opaque type `Name[+plane] <: Text`, with its companion. Its page under the
        `nomenclature` package has the full account; the companion's members, which the
        export does not declare, are documented here.
    entry .apply   `Name[plane](text)`: checks text against the plane's rules, raising `Name.Error`
        A `transparent inline` method which expands to the plane's rule checks, one after the
        other, followed by the text cast to `Name[plane]`. It needs a `plane is Nominative` given
        in scope — a missing one is a compile error, _Couldn't find a `Nominative` instance on
        `plane`_ — and a `Tactic[Name.Error]`, raised with the first rule which fails. Each
        rule's parameter must be a string literal type, as the `Must…` rules take.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        Name[CssClass](t"main-nav")
        capture[Name.Error](Name[CssClass](t"1col")).message.show
        // t"the name 1col is not valid because it must be a valid CSS identifier"
        ```
    entry .verify   `Name.verify[literal, plane]`: a literal checked against one plane as the code compiles
        An `inline` method which takes the name as a literal string type and the plane as a
        type, and either expands to the literal cast to `Name[plane]` or fails compilation with
        the rule's own words, _the name is not valid because it must not start with 0_. The
        `n"…"` interpolator is the usual way to write a checked literal; `verify` is the form for
        code which has the plane in hand and does not want the inference over every plane in
        scope which `n"…"` performs.
    entry .encodable   a name encodes as the text it is
        The `Encodable in Text` given for any `Name[plane]`: the identity, so a name serialises
        exactly as its text does.
    entry .inspectable   a name inspects as `n"…"`, distinguishable from the text it is
        The `Inspectable` given for any subtype of `Name[Any]`. A `Name` is a `Text`, so without
        an instance of its own it would reach the `Text` instance and render as `t"…"`, and a
        validated name would be indistinguishable from unchecked text; this one renders
        `n"foo"`, with the characters escaped as a text literal's are. It is written for every
        subtype of `Name[Any]` rather than for one plane, since the `Self` of a typeclass is
        invariant and an instance for `Name[Linux]` would not cover `Name[Linux & Posix]`.
    entry .decodable   `text.as[Name[plane]]`: validated decoding, through the `Decodable` typeclass
        An `inline` given, available for any `plane` with a `Nominative` given in scope, which
        decodes text by `Name[plane](text)`. It needs a `Tactic[Name.Error]` in scope, raised
        with the first rule the text fails, so any format which decodes its fields through
        `Decodable` — a JSON document, a command-line argument, a path element — can read a
        `Name[plane]` directly and reject an invalid one at the boundary.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        val id: Name[DomId] = t"main-content".as[Name[DomId]]
        ```
    entry .Error   raised when text is not a valid name in its plane
      detail
          Error 79: _the name `name` is not valid because it `must not start with 0`_. The
          second half is the violated rule's own description of its parameter
          (`Rule.describe`), so the message states the precise rule the text broke, not merely
          that it was invalid. Raised by `Name[plane](text)` and by `text.as[Name[plane]]` for
          the first rule in the plane's intersection which the text fails, and needs a
          `Diagnostics` in scope, such as `errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics`.

          ```scala
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

          capture[Name.Error](Name[Required](t"")).message.show
          // t"the name “” is not valid because it must not be empty"
          ```
      entry #name   the text which was rejected
      entry #rule   the `Rule` which rejected it, as `MustNotStart` or `CssIdentifier`
      entry #parameter   the rule's parameter: the literal its `Check` type was applied to
          For `MustNotStart["0"]` this is `0`; for a description-parameterised rule such as
          `CssIdentifier["a valid CSS identifier"]` it is the description.
    entry .Extractor   the object an `n"…"` literal expands to: a name by inference, or a pattern
      detail
          `n"hello"` expands to `Name.Extractor["hello"]()`, so this class is where both uses of
          the interpolator live: `#apply` makes the name, with the most precise plane the
          literal is valid in, and `#unapply` lets `n"hello"` be written as a pattern against a
          `Name[plane]`. It is not constructed directly.
      entry #apply   the literal as a `Name` of every plane in scope whose rules it satisfies
          A `transparent inline` method whose result type is the intersection of the `Self`
          types of every `Nominative` given in scope that accepts the literal, as
          `Name[A & B]`. A literal which no plane in scope accepts is a compile error, _the name
          `.` is not a valid identifier in any namespace in scope_. A plane whose rule has a
          non-literal parameter is skipped rather than reported.
      entry #unapply   `case n"main" =>`: matches a `Name[plane]` equal to the literal
          An `inline` extractor which checks, as the code compiles, that the literal is a valid
          name in the scrutinee's plane — a pattern which could never match is an error with
          the rule's words — and then compares the two texts at runtime.

entry .protointernal   the macro implementations behind `n"…"`, `Name[plane](…)` and `Name.verify`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home. Its public
      members are the macro bodies `extractor`, `inferName`, `makeName`, `parse`, `parse2` and
      `disintersection`, and the helpers they share: `build`, `decompose`, `everyNominative`,
      `planeAccepts`, `constant` and `companion`.
  entry .extractor   the macro behind `n"…"`: wraps the literal's type in a `Name.Extractor`
  entry .inferName   the macro behind `Name.Extractor#apply`: the intersection of every accepting plane in scope
      Collects every `Nominative` given in scope, keeps the `Self` of each whose rules all
      accept the literal, and splices the literal as a `Name` of their intersection; with no
      accepting plane it halts with _the name … is not a valid identifier in any namespace in
      scope_.
  entry .makeName   the macro behind `Name[plane](text)`: the plane's checks spliced in sequence
  entry .parse   the macro behind `Name.verify`: a literal checked against one plane, halting with the rule's words
  entry .parse2   the macro behind `Name.Extractor#unapply`: `parse`, then an equality test
  entry .disintersection   the macro behind `disintersect`
  entry .build   a list of types made into a tuple type
  entry .decompose   an intersection type taken apart into the set of its members
  entry .everyNominative   every `Nominative` given in scope at the expansion point, not only the best
      Runs the implicit search repeatedly, ignoring the givens already found and folding in
      both sides of an ambiguity, so that `n"…"` sees every plane in scope.
  entry .planeAccepts   whether every rule of a plane's `Limit` accepts a name
  entry .constant   the string a literal type stands for, with capture-checking annotations stripped
  entry .companion   the companion object of a symbol, loaded reflectively and typed as the caller asks

entry .anteprotointernal   the macro behind `staticCompanion`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: holds `staticCompanion`, the macro which resolves a type to its
      companion object, which `protointernal.makeName` uses to reach a `Rule` from a `Check`.
  entry .staticCompanion   the macro behind `nomenclature.staticCompanion`

entry .Moniker   a small integer with a memorable two-word name, `brilliant-leopard`, from a `Vocabulary`
  detail
      A `Moniker` is an `Int` ordinal paired, through its plane, with the `Vocabulary` which
      names it: `Moniker over Session` is a moniker whose words come from the
      `Vocabulary over Session` in scope. It encodes as `adjective-animal` and decodes from
      that text, so a session, a build or a job can be given a name a person can read back
      over the phone, while the program keeps the number. It lives in the
      `nomenclature.lexicon` source set.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      sealed trait Session
      given (Vocabulary over Session) = Vocabulary(cp"/nomenclature/adjectives.txt", cp"/nomenclature/animals.txt")

      Moniker[Session](10351).encode                      // t"brilliant-leopard"
      t"brilliant-leopard".as[Moniker over Session].ordinal   // 10351
      ```

      The plane is a type the program chooses, so two vocabularies can coexist as
      `Vocabulary over Session` and `Vocabulary over Job`, and `Moniker(10351)` with no plane
      written infers it from the single vocabulary in scope. Encoding and decoding both need
      a `Tactic[Moniker.Error]`, since a number can lie outside the vocabulary's range and
      text can name a word it does not have; a moniker *inspects* as its ordinal, `10351ᵐᵏ`,
      since a debug rendering can rely on neither the vocabulary nor a tactic.
  refer nomenclature.Vocabulary
  entry .Moniker   the opaque type itself, an `Int`, which `nomenclature` exports as `Moniker`
      `Moniker.Moniker` is declared inside the companion so that its representation as an
      `Int` is visible only there; the package exports it as `nomenclature.Moniker`, which is
      the name to write. Its plane is given by refinement, `Moniker over Session`.
  entry .apply   `Moniker[plane](ordinal)`: a moniker on the plane of the `Vocabulary` in scope
      Needs a `Vocabulary over plane` given; the plane is inferred from it when only one is
      in scope. The ordinal is not range-checked here, but when the moniker is encoded,
      which raises `Moniker.Error` with `OutOfRange` for an ordinal at or above the
      vocabulary's `size`.
  entry .ordinal   the integer a moniker stands for
      An extension on `Moniker`: the ordinal it was made from, or the one its text decoded
      to, `adjectiveIndex × animalCount + animalIndex`.
  entry .inspectable   a moniker inspects as its ordinal with a superscript suffix: `10351ᵐᵏ`
      The suffix distinguishes it from the `Int` it erases to. The rendering uses neither
      the vocabulary nor a tactic, which a debug rendering cannot assume to be in scope.
  entry .encodable   `moniker.encode`: the two-word name, through the `Vocabulary` in scope
      The `Encodable in Text` given for a `Moniker over plane`, needing a
      `Vocabulary over plane` and a `Tactic[Moniker.Error]`, raised with `OutOfRange` when the
      ordinal is not one the vocabulary can name. The given captures the tactic, so it is a
      capability and is resolved where the tactic is in scope.
  entry .decodable   `text.as[Moniker over plane]`: a two-word name read back as a moniker
      The `Decodable in Text` given for a `Moniker over plane`, needing a
      `Vocabulary over plane` and a `Tactic[Moniker.Error]`: `Malformed` is raised for text
      which is not two words joined by `-`, and `UnknownWord` for a word which is not in the
      vocabulary's list for its position.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      sealed trait Session
      given (Vocabulary over Session) = Vocabulary(cp"/nomenclature/adjectives.txt", cp"/nomenclature/animals.txt")

      capture[Moniker.Error](t"justoneword".as[Moniker over Session]).message.show
      // t"the moniker is not valid because justoneword is not of the form <adjective>-<animal>"
      ```
  entry .Error   raised when a moniker cannot be named or its text cannot be read
    detail
        Error 80, with a sub-number for its `#reason`: _the moniker is not valid because the
        number 100000 is outside the representable range_. Raised by `Moniker.encodable` for
        an ordinal the vocabulary cannot name, and by `Moniker.decodable` for text which is not
        `adjective-animal` or whose words the vocabulary does not have. Needs a `Diagnostics`
        in scope.
    entry #reason   why: `Unreadable`, `OutOfRange`, `Malformed` or `UnknownWord`
    entry .Reason   the four reasons a moniker is invalid, each with its own error sub-number
      detail
          Each case has its own error sub-number:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `Unreadable`  1  _the vocabulary could not be read_
          row  `OutOfRange(value)`  2  _the number `value` is outside the representable range_  an ordinal below zero, or at or above the vocabulary's `size`
          row  `Malformed(moniker)`  3  _`moniker` is not of the form `<adjective>-<animal>`_  text which does not split into exactly two words on `-`
          row  `UnknownWord(word)`  4  _the word `word` does not appear in the vocabulary_  a word missing from the adjective or animal list
          ```
      entry #number   the reason's sub-number, which `Moniker.Error` reports alongside error 80
    entry .communicable   renders each reason of a `Moniker.Error` as its message

entry .Vocabulary   two word lists, adjectives and animals, which name the monikers of a plane
  detail
      A `Vocabulary over plane` is built from two sources of words, one word per line, and
      names every ordinal from `0` to `#size - 1` as `adjective-animal`, taking the adjective
      at `ordinal / animals` and the animal at `ordinal % animals`. The sources are anything
      `Streamable by Data over Credit`: the word lists bundled with the `nomenclature.lexicon`
      component as the classpath resources `/nomenclature/adjectives.txt` (400 words) and
      `/nomenclature/animals.txt` (250 words), which name 100,000 monikers, or a program's
      own files.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      sealed trait Session
      val vocabulary: Vocabulary over Session = Vocabulary(cp"/nomenclature/adjectives.txt", cp"/nomenclature/animals.txt")
      vocabulary.size                   // 100000
      vocabulary.name(10351)            // t"brilliant-leopard"
      vocabulary.number(t"brilliant-leopard")   // 10351
      ```

      A vocabulary is bound as a given, `given (Vocabulary over Session) = …`, for
      `Moniker[Session](…)`, `encode` and `as[Moniker over Session]` to find; the plane is
      the program's own marker type, and the same two lists bound on two planes give two
      independent namespaces. Blank lines and surrounding whitespace in the sources are
      dropped; the order of the lines is the order of the ordinals, so a list must not be
      reordered once monikers have been stored.
  refer nomenclature.Moniker
  entry .apply   `Vocabulary(adjectives, animals)`: a vocabulary read from two streamable sources
      Reads each source as text, splits it on newlines, trims each line and drops the empty
      ones. The plane is the type parameter `transport`, inferred from the expected type
      `Vocabulary over Session`; the reads need the contextual values the sources' `Streamable`
      givens need, such as a `Classloader` for a classpath resource.
  entry #Transport   the plane the vocabulary names monikers on, written `Vocabulary over plane`
  entry #size   how many monikers the vocabulary can name: adjectives × animals
  entry #name   the two-word name of an ordinal, raising `Moniker.Error` if it is out of range
      `OutOfRange` is raised for an ordinal below zero or at or above `#size`. Needs a
      `Tactic[Moniker.Error]`.
  entry #number   the ordinal of a two-word name, raising `Moniker.Error` if it cannot be read
      Splits on `-` and looks each word up in its list: `Malformed` for text which is not
      exactly two words, `UnknownWord` for a word not in the list for its position. Needs a
      `Tactic[Moniker.Error]`.
